# THE RULE: anything a .gitignore file ignores is local to one machine, and must
# be excluded here too — `COPY . .` copies the build context, and git has no say
# in what that contains. CI enforces it without a second list to maintain:
# `.github/scripts/check-exposure.sh plant` writes a canary at the path of every
# git-ignore rule in the repository, and `check-exposure.sh layers` fails if any
# canary reaches any image layer. A new git-ignore rule therefore fails CI until
# it is mirrored here.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.editorconfig
tests
storage/logs
# Laravel's runtime state: caches, compiled views, sessions, test disks and the
# maintenance-mode `down` file. The Dockerfile recreates the directories it needs.
storage/framework
# Every .env variant. Runtime configuration is injected by the platform; an image
# that carries a .env would override it (Laravel reads .env before the process
# environment) and would bake credentials into a distributable layer.
.env
.env.*
# THE PASSPORT SIGNING KEYS. `php artisan passport:keys` writes these into
# storage/ during local setup, and .gitignore keeps them out of git — but git is
# not the build context. `COPY . .` was therefore baking a developer's private
# signing key into a distributable image layer on every local `docker build`,
# where it survives every later deletion and is readable by anyone who can pull
# the image. CI never noticed: a fresh checkout has no key files, so the layer
# was clean there and dirty on exactly the machines that had a key to leak.
#
# Excluding them is also what makes the failure LOUD rather than silent. With no
# key files in the image, a deployment that forgot to inject
# PASSPORT_PRIVATE_KEY/PASSPORT_PUBLIC_KEY is refused at startup by
# app:check-config, instead of quietly signing tokens with a key from a laptop.
storage/*.key
# A developer's framework caches. `php artisan config:cache` (or `optimize`) in the
# bind-mounted dev container writes bootstrap/cache/config.php holding every
# RESOLVED .env value — database, Redis and mail credentials, APP_KEY — and it is
# git-ignored, so nothing else keeps it out of a local build. The Dockerfile
# recreates the directory; composer and docker/entrypoint.sh regenerate the rest.
bootstrap/cache/*
# A developer's local uploads and scratch files. The Dockerfile recreates the
# directory; nothing durable may live in the image anyway.
storage/app
# A local SQLite file (and its -journal/-wal siblings), created by composer's
# post-create-project script. Never used by this template (the engine is
# PostgreSQL) and it can carry real rows.
database/*.sqlite*
docker-compose.yml
# Only the PostgreSQL data volumes — docker/entrypoint.sh and docker/healthcheck.sh
# are COPYed into the image and must stay in the build context.
docker/database/
# The Dockerfile installs dependencies from composer.lock with --no-dev. A host
# vendor/ would otherwise put every dev package into the COPY layer underneath.
vendor
# Local-only files that can hold credentials or data: Composer registry
# credentials, ad-hoc backups and dumps, editor and VM settings.
**/auth.json
**/*.bak
**/.phpactor.json
**/Homestead.json
**/Homestead.yaml
**/.DS_Store
**/supervisord.pid
.fleet
.vscode
*.md
start.sh
stop.sh
test.sh
test-pipeline.sh
# Tooling and generated artifacts with no role at runtime. Excluding them keeps
# the context small and keeps CI config, editor settings and the generated
# OpenAPI document out of the shipped image.
.github
.claude
.junie
.idea
.zap
.trivyignore
.phpunit.result.cache
.phpunit.cache
phpunit.xml
api.json
boost.json
public/storage
